From 8ecf7ef242bae8e5ee61de7acf88932a035624bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 11:30:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] SessionDB: memoize the schema init per settled
 store; delegate-marker heal off the per-open path
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Design t_01a1d45d75851804 §2d (card t_01a1d47170ff4fc5). Hub py-spy: `SessionDB.__init__` ->
`_open_writer` -> `_init_schema` was 6.9% of a steady serve's samples (heal 4.6%) and 3.9% of the
gateway's. Every short-lived writable handle reran SCHEMA_SQL, the column reconcile, the PK heals and
`_heal_reset_rows_carrying_a_delegate_marker`, a full scan of ~23k `sessions` rows (~14 ms).

What changes (new `hermes_state_schema_memo.py`; `_init_schema` wraps the old body, now
`_init_schema_full`):

- Process-level memo keyed (realpath, st_dev, st_ino stat'd after connect, PRAGMA schema_version, the
  `schema_version` table value, store_instance_id, a digest of SCHEMA_SQL/FTS DDL/SCHEMA_VERSION and the
  migration module source, the connection class). A full init is recorded only when it left the store
  settled: no `fts_stale`, schema at this build's SCHEMA_VERSION, message_uid backfill and legacy-refusal
  repair done, no open transaction, and every attribute it set on `self` a plain scalar.
- A hit restores the attributes the first init set (`_fts_enabled`, `_trigram_available`, `_fts_stale`
  plus any other changed scalar, found by diffing `vars(self)` around the full init) after re-probing
  both FTS tables with `LIMIT 0` reads; disagreement, any probe error or lock falls back to the full init
  (whose lock patience owns the wait).
- `fts_stale` is read from `state_meta` on every open; set by another handle, the open takes the full
  init and its stale-FTS recovery.
- A replaced/quarantined file (new inode), DDL by another process (PRAGMA schema_version) or a moved
  data-migration level (schema_version table) reruns the full init.
- The delegate-marker heal runs with the full init (the first open per key per process), otherwise from
  the memo-hit open at most once per HEAL_INTERVAL_S = 600 s per store (claimed under a lock before it
  runs; a sqlite error is logged and retried next interval, never fails the open).

Tests (tests/hermes_state/test_schema_init_memo.py), RED on origin/main -> GREEN:
second open skips init (spy on `_reconcile_columns`), cached flags restored (and search works on the
restored handle), `fts_stale` set by another handle seen on the next open, heal throttled to one per
interval and the throttled heal still repairs a polluted row. Guards that also hold on main: inode swap
reruns, external ALTER TABLE reruns, downgraded schema_version table reruns migrations, a runtime whose
trigram probe fails does not inherit the flag. test_reset_row_delegate_marker's two reopen-after-surgery
tests now clear the memo first (the heal runs on a process's first open, as there).
tests/hermes_state/ full run: green apart from 3 failures fixed by the probe check / connection-class key
(test_write_lock_patience writer race, TestFtsRebuildFinishWithoutTrigram x2), then those 4 files
319 passed. scripts/check --base origin/main: ok.

Micro-bench (hermes-worker-5, 23,000-row `sessions`, N=200 sequential `SessionDB(path).close()` after
one warm open; same interpreter):
  origin/main: 4.263 s total, 21.32 ms/open
  this change: 2.772 s total, 13.86 ms/open
cProfile, 100 opens: `_init_schema` cumulative 8.2 ms/open (heal 5.4 ms) -> 0.49 ms/open. Most of
what remains per open is `refuse_deleted_wal_generation`'s system-wide /proc holder scan (~19 ms/open
under the profiler), outside this card's scope.
